<!--Post: 1295--><!--Time: 1098891000-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST1295"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:hackenmaster@cox.net" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Wichitan<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Wichitan<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->10-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, October 27, 2004 - 8:30 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->There is no &#34;commmon&#34; number for line 4 or 6.  I have to say that I have ran across alot of numerical theories, take for instance: <BR> <BR>From: 1684 S. Oldmanor  <BR>To: 825 E. Douglas  <BR>Sept &#40;16&#41;, 1986 Vicki Wegerle attacked  <BR>1684 - 825 = 859  <BR> <BR>859 - 16 = 843  <BR> <BR>843 S. Pershing - Nancy Fox <BR> <BR>But, when you tell me that there is a common number such as 2,3, then what about line  4 and 6, what is common about these numbers?  I&#39;m not trying to doubt what you are saying, I just want a better understanding of it.  Lets face facts math is exact, and it can be misrepresented, much like: <BR> <BR>3 guys pay 10 dollars for a hotel room. <BR> <BR>the clerk made a mistake and charged 5 dollars to much. <BR> <BR>sent the bell hope up to return the refund, bell hope thought how are they gonna split 5 dollars 3 ways? <BR> <BR>He keeps 2 dollars, gives each of the guys 1 dollar. <BR> <BR>now the math <BR> <BR>$30 represents total price of the room <BR> <BR>they each got a dollar back. <BR> <BR>3X9=$27&#43;$2&#40;for the bell hop&#41;=$29 <BR> <BR>If mathematical equasions are misrepresented, then there is no exact answer. <BR> <BR>for the record: <BR>$25 for the room <BR>$1 for each guy <BR>$2 for the bell hope <BR>---------------- <BR>$30 <BR> <BR>wichitan<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 1295-->
<!--Post: 1297--><!--Time: 1098892551-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST1297"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:hackenmaster@cox.net" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Wichitan<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Wichitan<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->10-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, October 27, 2004 - 8:55 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I suppose the real question here is where Tom got the grid from.  And whatsmore is it here to play with our minds or is it something factual? <BR> <BR>your killing us Tom, <BR> <BR>wichitan<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 1297-->
<!--Post: 1298--><!--Time: 1098892845-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST1298"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Sean<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Sean<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, October 27, 2004 - 9:00 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I think you have misunderstood me. The whole system is based on a simple equation called Fadic reduction. Basically what that means is that all numbers with more than one digit must be reduced to a single number/digit. <BR> <BR>Line 4 gives D,M and V respectively.&#40;D= 4&#41; &#40;M= 13=1&#43;3=4&#41;&#40;v=22= 2&#43;2=4&#41;. Similarily line 6 gives FO and X &#40;F=6&#41; &#40;O=15 =1&#43;5=6&#41; &#40;x=24 =2&#43;4=6&#41;. <BR>That&#39;s how this Numerological table was designed. <BR> <BR>Another example would be say, the year of the murders 1974 = 1&#43;9&#43;7&#43;4 = 21=2&#43;1=3. <BR> <BR>Since BTK has apparently given this clue, it might have employed in either the choice of victim <BR>or he may have given a number that corresponds to his own name. But we do not know what his exact thinging was.It could be linked to names,addresses,liscence numbers,phone numbers, DOB&#39;s etc, or a combination. <BR>Interesting perhaps that he once wrote that he &#34;took down their numbers and followed them home&#34;. <BR>I fully agree that anything like this must have a strict math base/formula. I can&#39;t account for other numerical theories,but this appears to be a direct clue because that is the standard Numerical table which has been around for centuries. <BR> <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 1298-->
<!--Post: 1299--><!--Time: 1098893904-->
